## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design scalable multi-tenant data isolation using AWS IAM, Lake Formation, and S3 policies across multiple regions. It explores understanding of cross-region replication, governance controls, and auditability for SaaS applications.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped IAM roles with STS AssumeRole session tags
- Lake Formation permissions and data lake zone isolation
- S3 replication, encryption (SSE-KMS), and access boundaries
- Least-privilege access enforcement through bucket policies
- Auditing with CloudTrail, AWS Config, and Access Analyzer

## Code Example
```javascript
// Trust policy snippet for tenant role
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::${tenant_account_id}:role/TenantUser"},
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
    "Condition": {
      "StringEquals": {"aws:RequestTag/tenant-id": "${tenant_id}"},
      "StringLike": {"aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "${tenant_id}"}
    }
  }]
}

// S3 bucket policy for tenant isolation
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Deny",
    "Principal": "*",
    "Action": "s3:*",
    "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::datalake-bucket/*",
    "Condition": {
      "StringNotEquals": {"s3:prefix": ["${tenant_id}/*"]},
      "Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}
    }
  }]
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to architect scalable, secure data ingestion across accounts with governance at scale, matching enterprise needs from Databricks and Salesforce.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account IAM roles and resource policies
- Lake Formation governance and Glue Data Catalog
- Per-tenant S3 isolation and partitioning strategy
- KMS key management and envelope encryption
- Audit/logging with CloudTrail, Config, and Lake Formation logs

## Code Example

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-*-data-lake/*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalTag/tenant_id": "${aws:userid}"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Interview Strategy

Focus on demonstrating enterprise-scale architecture patterns, emphasizing security isolation, governance automation, and cost optimization across multi-tenant environments.

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design scalable, resilient streaming pipelines on AWS, balancing compute warm-start strategies, data correctness (exactly-once), deployment safety, and observability under real-world load spikes.

## Key Concepts

- Lambda provisioned concurrency
- Kinesis Enhanced Fan-Out
- Idempotent processing and deduplication
- Dead-letter queues (DLQ)
- Canary/Blue-Green deployment with CodeDeploy
- Observability (CloudWatch, X-Ray)
- Data durability in S3 with versioning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-idempotent handler
function handle(record) {
  const id = record.eventSourceARN + ':' + record.sequenceNumber;
  if (cache.has(id)) return;
  // process
  storeRecord(record);
  cache.set(id, true);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement exactly-once semantics across DynamoDB and S3 writes?
- What metrics signal a regression after deployment, and how would you respond?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical AWS workflow skills: serverless orchestration with Step Functions, Lambda, and DynamoDB; emphasizes least privileges, observability, and basic fault tolerance suitable for junior-level candidates.

## Key Concepts

- Step Functions state machine design
- Lambda execution roles and IAM least privilege
- S3-triggered processing and idempotent writes
- Retry/backoff, failure handling, CloudWatch logging

## Code Example

```javascript
// ValidateHeader Lambda (JS)
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const expected = ['id','name','email'];
  const header = event.headers || [];
  const ok = expected.every(h => header.includes(h));
  if (!ok) throw new Error('Invalid header');
  return { valid: true };
};
```

```javascript
// TransformRows Lambda (JS)
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const records = event.records || [];
  return records.map(r => ({ ...r, json: JSON.stringify(r) }));
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement idempotency to avoid duplicate writes?
- What changes would you make to handle CSVs with large row counts?
- How would you test permissions with least privilege in a fresh account?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of end-to-end event-driven AWS patterns, from S3 events to Lambda processing to DynamoDB storage, with proper IAM scoping and TLS enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- S3 Event Notifications
- Lambda execution roles (least privilege)
- DynamoDB PutItem permissions
- S3 bucket policy TLS enforcement
- Security without public access and basic end-to-end testing

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::company-logs/app1/*"
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["dynamodb:PutItem"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:dynamodb:REGION:ACCOUNT_ID:table/App1LogSummary"
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::company-logs/app1/*",
      "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "false"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate resilience against Lambda timeouts or large files?
- How would you extend this to multiple app prefixes with isolation rules?
